Hash speed on Intel(R) Xeon(R) Gold 6330 CPU @ 2.00GHz (linux7.0.0-38-generic, 112 CPUs), 2026-11-04 01:10:01 UTC
Full run: 96 rounds. Each cell is the mean time per unit (total time over total work), lower is better.

In “A message in one buffer, after other work”, “A batch of 64-byte messages, after other work”, “A batch of 4 KiB messages, after other work”, and “Many messages at once, a turn of their pieces after other work”, the program hashes, runs other code and reads 128 MiB of memory (at least 1 ms), and hashes again, as a program hashes between its other tasks; the second call, with the write of its input, is timed.
In “A message in one buffer, after idling”, “A batch of 64-byte messages, after idling”, and “Many messages at once, a turn of their pieces after idling”, the program hashes, sleeps 1 ms, and hashes again, as a server handles a request, waits, and handles the next; the second call, with the write of its input, is timed.
In “Messages one after another, buffers owned”, “Batches one after another, buffers owned”, “Messages one after another, buffers lent”, “Many messages at once, each arriving in pieces, buffers lent”, “A collection's messages, each hashed once, in memory”, “Batches one after another, buffers lent”, “A collection's messages one after another, each read into a buffer, then hashed”, and “A Merkle tree over leaves just written into place, every layer kept”, the program hashes one input after another, as fast as it can, alone and with a second program doing the same at once.

SOLO: one copy of each contender, on one thread.

